msgid "Use `Alt+Enter` for available code actions—rust-analyzer provides many"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/rustrover.md:165
msgid ""
"Configure rust-analyzer settings in `.zed/settings.json` for project-"
"specific needs"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/rustrover.md:166
msgid ""
"Run `cargo clippy` for linting (it integrates with rust-analyzer diagnostics)"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/rustrover.md:168
msgid "Project Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/rustrover.md:170
msgid ""
"Both editors store per-project configuration in a hidden folder. RustRover "
"uses `.idea` (with XML files), Zed uses `.zed` (with JSON files)."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/rustrover.md:172
msgid ""
"**Run configurations don't transfer.** RustRover stores run/debug "
"configurations in `.idea`. These have no automatic migration path. You'll "
"recreate them as Zed [tasks](../tasks.md) in `.zed/tasks.json` and debug "
"configurations in `.zed/debug.json`."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/rustrover.md:174
msgid ""
"**No Cargo tool window.** RustRover provides a visual tree of your workspace "
"members, targets, features, and dependencies. Zed doesn't have this. You "
"work with `Cargo.toml` and the Cargo CLI directly."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/rustrover.md:176
msgid ""
"**Toolchain management is external.** RustRover lets you select and switch "
"toolchains in its settings UI. In Zed, you manage toolchains through "
"`rustup`."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/rustrover.md:178
msgid ""
"**Configuration is opt-in.** RustRover auto-generates `.idea` when you open "
"a project. Zed doesn't generate anything. You create `.zed/settings.json`, "
"`tasks.json`, and `debug.json` as needed."
msgstr ""

#: src/migrate/rustrover.md:182
msgid ""
"Create a `.zed/settings.json` in your project root for project-specific "
"settings"
msgstr ""

